# Copy this file to .env and uncomment what you want to change.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Every value below is the built-in default, so an untouched copy changes nothing.
# Variables already set in the real environment win over this file — a value passed
# to docker compose, or set for a single run, is not overridden by a stale .env.
#
# Docker reads this file too, but on the host: compose substitutes ${VAR} into its
# own environment block. The file is deliberately kept out of the image.

# --- Serving -----------------------------------------------------------------

# PORT=8787
# HOST=0.0.0.0

# Origins allowed to call the API. Only relevant if you serve the web app from a
# different origin than the API; the single-container deployment does not.
# CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173,http://localhost:4173

# trace | debug | info | warn | error | fatal
# LOG_LEVEL=info

# --- Access ------------------------------------------------------------------

# none | password | multiuser
# multiuser is not implemented: the server starts and then refuses every request.
# AUTH_MODE=none

# Required when AUTH_MODE=password. No default, deliberately.
# AUTH_PASSWORD=

# --- Storage -----------------------------------------------------------------

# One SQLite file. Backing up means copying it.
# DATABASE_PATH=./data/dwc.db

# --- Measurement -------------------------------------------------------------

# Explicit resolvers, never the host's. A local resolver that refuses queries
# would otherwise make every measurement a property of your machine.
# DNS_RESOLVERS=1.1.1.1,8.8.8.8,9.9.9.9

# Requests per run used to judge consistency. Below 5, variance is noise and the
# engine refuses to draw a conclusion from it.
# STABILITY_SAMPLES=5

# Where the browser measures its latency baseline.
#
# Unset means same-origin. On a local install that baseline is loopback, which
# says nothing about anyone's internet — so "your connection" and "the path
# between" correctly report as not measured rather than inventing a number.
#
# Any URL your browser can reach over the internet will do. The round trip is
# timed without reading the response, so the far end grants nothing and does not
# need to know this tool exists. A local, private or link-local address is
# refused at boot.
#
# The whole URL is used, path included. Point it at a cheap, empty endpoint
# rather than at a site's front page: a home page is hundreds of kilobytes and
# its transfer time would be reported as your latency.
#
#   CONTROL_URL=https://www.gstatic.com/generate_204
#
# That endpoint returns an empty 204 and exists for connectivity checks, so it is
# cheap to call. It is a static host, so there are no cookies and no redirect to
# a country domain. It is a third party, and nothing here contacts one unless you
# uncomment this line.
#
# For reference, measured from Cape Town on a 100 Mb line — any well-peered
# endpoint near you will look similar, and the point is that they are all in the
# same range, whereas a front page is not:
#
#   https://www.gstatic.com/generate_204        28 ms
#   https://www.google.com/generate_204         30 ms
#   https://cloudflare.com/cdn-cgi/trace        15 ms
#   https://www.google.com          (no path)  519 ms   <- the whole home page
#
# How far it gets you depends on what you point it at:
#
#   any endpoint       "your connection" becomes measurable
#   another instance   "the path between" becomes measurable as well
#
# The route needs a second instance because judging it means subtracting your
# link's cost from your time to the site, and that subtraction assumes the two
# are comparable measurements. A large provider answers from whichever edge is
# nearest you, so the baseline comes out short by however far the site actually
# is — and the leftover, which the report would blame on your provider, is really
# just distance. REFERENCE_URLS below is the other way to get a route verdict,
# and it needs nothing of your own deployment.
#
# A paired instance needs your origin in its CORS_ORIGINS. This server never
# fetches the URL; only your browser does.
# CONTROL_URL=

# --- Behind a proxy or a CDN -------------------------------------------------

# Whether to believe X-Forwarded-For.
#
# Off unless set, and that default matters: with it on and nothing actually in
# front, any client can pick its own value and mint a fresh rate-limit bucket per
# request. Turn it on only when a proxy you control is guaranteed to be in front —
# which is exactly the deployment DEPLOYMENT.md describes. Without it, every
# visitor behind your proxy shares one bucket.
#
# Accepts true, a hop count, or a comma-separated list of trusted addresses.
# TRUST_PROXY=true

# Whether this instance sits behind a CDN edge.
#
# Detected per request from headers only a distributed frontend sets, so you
# normally leave this alone. It decides whether the route verdict may run: behind
# an edge the visitor's connection ends near them and never reaches this machine,
# so the latency baseline is too short to subtract and the report says the route
# cannot be judged rather than blaming somebody's provider for distance.
#
# Set it if you are behind a frontend we do not recognise, or if we misread yours.
# EDGE_TERMINATED=true

# Public endpoints the browser times alongside the target.
#
# The other way to get a route verdict, and the only one that needs nothing of
# your own deployment — it works behind a CDN, behind a tunnel, and on a laptop.
# The browser times these over the same link within seconds of the target; the
# quickest is the reader's floor, and what the target costs above it is what
# reaching that site costs them.
#
# Third parties, so nothing is contacted unless you set this.
# REFERENCE_URLS=https://www.google.com/generate_204,https://cloudflare.com/cdn-cgi/trace

# --- Limits ------------------------------------------------------------------

# Diagnostics per client IP per minute. This is the one that matters: a probe
# opens connections to a third party on a stranger's behalf.
# RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE=20

# Everything else per client IP per minute. A runaway-client backstop, not a
# throttle on reading — page loads and report views spend from this.
# READS_PER_MINUTE=600

# MAX_REDIRECTS=10
# MAX_RESPONSE_BYTES=5000000

# --- Timeouts (milliseconds) -------------------------------------------------

# TIMEOUT_DNS_MS=5000
# TIMEOUT_CONNECT_MS=8000
# TIMEOUT_TLS_MS=8000
# TIMEOUT_HTTP_MS=15000
# TIMEOUT_TOTAL_MS=45000

# --- Development only --------------------------------------------------------

# Where the Vite dev server proxies /api. Read by apps/web/vite.config.ts at
# startup, not by the API, and irrelevant to the built app or the container.
# API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8787
